{"envelope": "{\"format\":\"gravitas.artifact\",\"formatVersion\":1,\"id\":\"compute:periodograms:cdec1936f69a2cdb\",\"made\":{\"app\":\"gravitas\"},\"provenance\":{\"citations\":[\"TESS Light Curves - All Sectors, STScI/MAST\",\"Ricker et al. 2015, JATIS 1, 014003 (TESS)\",\"Jenkins et al. 2016, Proc. SPIE 9913, 99133E (SPOC)\"],\"credit\":\"TESS, sector 56 (NASA; SPOC light curve from MAST)\",\"license\":{\"statement\":\"NASA mission data, released through MAST without restriction on reuse. MAST asks that work using its data acknowledge the mission and the archive.\",\"status\":\"public-domain\"},\"reductions\":[\"QUALITY: any nonzero flag drops the cadence (1288 dropped)\",\"TIME, PDCSAP_FLUX, PDCSAP_FLUX_ERR: a cadence without a finite value in all three is dropped (0 dropped)\",\"bin: a bin with fewer than 3 kept cadences is dropped (3 dropped)\",\"20-minute bins average 10 two-minute cadences each, which rounds ingress and egress to a bin.\",\"Flux is kept to 1 ppm and its error to 5 ppm.\",\"Normalized by the median. Lomb-Scargle with the error bars as weights (astropy.timeseries.LombScargle), ten samples per peak between 0.5 day and the longest period; false-alarm probability by the naive formula for the number of independent frequencies.\",\"Box least squares (astropy.timeseries.BoxLeastSquares), durations 1 to 6 hours, a coarse grid then a fine grid across the best peak, scored by the likelihood; the epoch of the best box is barycentric Julian date 2459826.78542 (TDB).\",\"The depth's sigma is the box search's, rescaled by the box model's reduced chi-square when it exceeds 1; the signal-to-noise ratio is the depth over that sigma.\"],\"retrieved\":\"2026-09-24\"},\"quantities\":[{\"id\":\"ls.period\",\"origin\":\"measured\",\"uncertainty\":{\"kind\":\"none\"},\"unit\":\"d\",\"value\":1.7689053410893705},{\"id\":\"ls.power\",\"origin\":\"measured\",\"uncertainty\":{\"kind\":\"none\"},\"unit\":\"\",\"value\":0.0723270909274195},{\"id\":\"ls.fap\",\"origin\":\"derived\",\"uncertainty\":{\"kind\":\"none\"},\"unit\":\"\",\"value\":1.2972221464763617e-29},{\"id\":\"bls.period\",\"origin\":\"measured\",\"uncertainty\":{\"kind\":\"none\"},\"unit\":\"d\",\"value\":3.523958475735776},{\"id\":\"bls.epoch\",\"origin\":\"measured\",\"uncertainty\":{\"kind\":\"none\"},\"unit\":\"d\",\"value\":2826.7854166666666},{\"id\":\"bls.duration\",\"origin\":\"measured\",\"uncertainty\":{\"kind\":\"none\"},\"unit\":\"h\",\"value\":2.5},{\"id\":\"bls.depth\",\"origin\":\"measured\",\"uncertainty\":{\"basis\":\"scaled\",\"kind\":\"sigma\",\"sigma\":76.27127463369673},\"unit\":\"ppm\",\"value\":14581.801026000436},{\"id\":\"bls.snr\",\"origin\":\"derived\",\"uncertainty\":{\"kind\":\"none\"},\"unit\":\"\",\"value\":191.1833923850301},{\"id\":\"period.ratio\",\"origin\":\"derived\",\"uncertainty\":{\"kind\":\"none\"},\"unit\":\"\",\"value\":0.5019654326999515}],\"source\":{\"digest\":\"cdec1936f69a2cdb1a0c8ae4e0e4cc641c38e433b2d44afadd032768036d1c4f\",\"id\":\"compute/periodograms\",\"kind\":\"analysis\",\"version\":\"1\"},\"warnings\":[\"a periodogram's best period has no error bar here: it is the best point of a grid, and the peak's sharpness depends on the grid, the durations and the box's shape. The BLS period is good to about the fine grid's step (3e-07 d) and no better\",\"Lomb-Scargle fits sinusoids: a transit is not one, so its tallest peak is not the planet's period, and the false-alarm probability says there is a periodic signal and not that the period is right\",\"a periodogram cannot tell the planet's period from half or twice it: the dips have to be counted\",\"the epoch is in the light curve's own time (BTJD = BJD - 2457000)\"]}", "table": "{\"annotations\":[],\"axes\":{\"x\":\"period\",\"y\":\"bls\"},\"citations\":[{\"text\":\"TESS Light Curves - All Sectors, STScI/MAST\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.17909/t9-nmc8-f686\"},{\"text\":\"Ricker et al. 2015, JATIS 1, 014003 (TESS)\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.1117/1.JATIS.1.1.014003\